<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tuoba</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tabgach, Tuoba clan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tuoba were a clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> people who founded the Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Wei dynasty</a>, which ruled northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from 386 to 534. Moving south from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-khingan">Greater Khingan</a> region, they established the state of Dai in the fourth century and reunified northern China by 439. Their name, as Tabgach, was used by the Turks for China, and Byzantine writers knew China as Taugast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tuoba.jpg" alt="Tuoba" width="320" height="578" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gary Todd · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Northern_Wei_soldier.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Tuoba</b>, or Tabgach, were a clan of the Xianbei confederation, whose state modern historians see as a synthesis of Chinese and nomadic traditions that rapidly sinicised.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-wei">Northern Wei · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Cheluhui organised them as a tribe, which took the name Rouran and paid tribute to the <b>Tuoba</b> state of Dai.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rouran-khaganate">Rouran Khaganate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 873 <b>Tuoba</b> Sigong seized Youzhou; for helping drive Huang Chao from Chang'an he received in 881 the four prefectures of the Dingnan Jiedushi and in 883 the imperial surname Li and the title Duke of Xia, a frontier command his house held through the Five Dynasties.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-xia">Western Xia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Tuoba</b> founded the state of Dai and then the Northern Wei, which unified northern China in 439, patronised the Buddhist cave temples of Yungang and Longmen, and under Emperor Xiaowen moved its capital to Luoyang in 494 and adopted Chinese dress, language and surnames.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…state its historical setting, but its geographic and cultural references point to the Northern Wei, founded by the <b>Tuoba clan</b> of the Xianbei, a proto-Mongolic people from the northern steppes who united northern China in the 4th century and moved their capital from Pingcheng (modern Datong) to Luoyang.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ballad-of-mulan">The Ballad of Mulan</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Tuoba</b> were a Xianbei confederation who moved into the area of modern Hohhot.</blockquote><a href="/state/dai-sixteen-kingdoms">Dai (Sixteen Kingdoms)</a></li></ul></section>
